WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.094
Low-light cameras must trade among sensitivity, exposure time, noise, motion blur, and detail.

00:00:06.094 --> 00:00:11.768
Increasing ISO or electronic gain can brighten an image while amplifying noise.

00:00:11.768 --> 00:00:18.282
A slower shutter can gather more light but blur moving subjects and aircraft motion.

00:00:18.282 --> 00:00:23.956
Automatic camera settings may produce an attractive preview that lacks reliable evidence.

00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:29.630
Dark areas can lose detail, highlights can clip, and color can shift.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:36.564
Review a sample and match settings to the mission rather than assuming brightness equals quality.

00:00:36.564 --> 00:00:46.441
Thermal imaging can reveal heat differences under suitable conditions, but it does not see through every material or identify cause by itself.

00:00:46.441 --> 00:00:51.274
Surface properties, distance, weather, calibration, and time influence apparent temperature.

00:00:51.274 --> 00:01:01.571
Spotlights or illumination can improve a limited scene but may create glare, shadows, wildlife or community disturbance, privacy concerns, and additional power demand.

00:01:01.571 --> 00:01:07.245
Use only when lawful, necessary, and compatible with the mission and equipment.

00:01:07.245 --> 00:01:10.397
Match sensor capability to the question.

00:01:10.397 --> 00:01:19.853
If the mission requires detail the payload cannot capture reliably at night, reschedule for daylight or use a different authorized method.

00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:24.686
Do not lower evidentiary standards because the flight was difficult.

00:01:24.686 --> 00:01:26.577
Report image limitations.

00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:33.512
Noise, blur, compression, low contrast, missing metadata, and uncertain scale should remain visible in analysis.

00:01:33.512 --> 00:01:38.345
Poor imagery cannot support confident identity, intent, or condition judgments.
